      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended on this curve, y equals three to the two x, and two other composites we haven't done yet. Composites hide in everyday things. A drone hovers, and its camera tilts forward at a steady rate. The spot it films races ahead along the ground, and its speed comes from two places: how fast the camera tilts, and how far the spot moves for each bit of tilt. Keep that picture. Here are two quick answers for the derivative of three to the two x. One says two times three to the two x. The other says three to the two x, times the natural log of three. At x equals zero, the first predicts a slope of two, and the second about one point one zero. Let's measure the real slope. Secants from closer and closer points give four, then about two point four six, then about two point two two. They settle at about two point two. Neither answer is right. So what is the slope, and why does a composite like this need more than one factor?"
    },
