      "costUsd": 0.013212,
      "pronunciationText": "So every composite needs two separate things: the outside's slope, worked out at the inside, and the inside's own slope. Here are the three outsides we haven't used yet. ay to the power of an inside: copy it, times the natural log of ay, times the inside's derivative. Log base ay of an inside: the inside's derivative, over the inside times the natural log of ay, and the inside must be positive. Tan of an inside: sec squared of the same inside, times the inside's derivative, and it breaks wherever the inside reaches pi over two, plus k pi. For the drone, the spot's distance is tan of the tilt, so its speed is sec squared of the tilt, times the tilt's own rate. And once the tilt reaches pi over two, the camera sees only the horizon. The outside brings its own fixed part, the natural log of ay, or sec squared. The inside's derivative is an extra factor, and the inside also decides the domain."
    },
